{{Politics of Okaiken}}
The {{Nihongo|'''59th general election of members of the House of Commoners'''|第59回衆議院議員総選挙|dai-gōjūkyūkai Shūgiin giin sōsenkyo}} was held on 25 July 2021, following the dissolution of the House of Commoners. Voting took place in all [[List of districts of the House of Commoners of Japan|Representatives constituencies]] of [[Okaiken]] to appoint [[List of members of the Diet of Okaiken|Members of Diet]] to all 475 seats in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]], the [[wikipedia:lower house|lower house]] of the [[Diet of Okaiken|National Diet of Okaiken]].

The election resulted in the defeat of the incumbent left-wing coalition government. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), led by [[Honda Shigetsuma]], won a plurality of seats, becoming the largest party in the Diet, but fell short of securing an outright majority. The [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP) of incumbent Prime Minister [[Sho Aoyagi]] suffered heavy losses, falling to second place. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) stagnated, remaining in third place. Following the election, Honda Shigetsuma formed a minority government, becoming Prime Minister on August 25, 2021.

==Background==
The 58th Diet was formed by a "left-wing" coalition government between [[Sho Aoyagi]]'s [[Sanese Communist Party]] and [[Honda Shigetsuma]]'s [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], which had formed to oust the long-ruling LDP in 2016. However, the coalition proved fragile, with significant ideological divides between the hard-left SCP and the centrist-progressive PPO.

The Aoyagi government struggled to manage the economy, facing the worst levels of inflation in 2020, which was compounded by a notable lack of foreign investment and business uncertainty over the government's economic policies.

A major gaffe occurred in early 2021 during a Diet questioning period when Prime Minister Sho Aoyagi fumbled a question from the opposition about military expenses. He inadvertently admitted that his government had, in fact, increased defense spending by 1.5% to modernize forces, despite explicit 2016 campaign promises to reduce the military budget. This admission alienated his party's pacifist base and provided potent attack material for the opposition.

Meanwhile, the opposition [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] faced its own internal turmoil. A leadership recall election was forced against party leader [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]] in the spring of 2021. This forced Tanizaki to focus on internal party politics rather than the upcoming general election. While he ultimately won the leadership vote, the event deeply fractured his party and damaged its public image as a stable alternative, with polling showing voters were wary of the "continued squabbling."

Riding this wave of widespread discontent, the Progressive Party, headed by Honda Shigetsuma (a third-generation politician from the Honda family), positioned itself as a stable, centrist alternative to both the incumbent Communists and the fractured LDP. The PPO gained significant momentum, with Honda's popularity soaring in the months leading up to the election.

==Election campaign==
The 45-day official campaign period was dominated by widespread voter anxiety over the economy and a deep-seated frustration with the incumbent coalition. Polling by KTK and the ''Asahi Okaiken'' showed that "inflation and cost of living" (42%) and "economic stability" (31%) were the top two issues for voters, far surpassing "social reform" (8%) and "foreign policy" (5%).

The campaign was largely seen as a three-way contest between the PPO, SCP, and LDP, though it became clear that the primary battle was between the two ruling coalition partners, who spent more time attacking each other's platforms than the opposition.

===The PPO's "Stable Progress" Platform===
Honda's PPO ran on a platform of "Stable Progress," a slogan designed to acknowledge the social reforms of the past five years while simultaneously promising a return to competent economic management.

Honda's performance in the first televised leaders' debate on July 2 was seen as a turning point. When pressed by the moderator on how his government would be different, Honda famously looked directly at the camera and said, "My partner Prime Minister Aoyagi believes in revolution. I believe in stability. You cannot pay for your groceries with a revolution." This soundbite dominated the next day's news cycle and cemented the PPO's image as the pragmatic, adult choice.

The PPO's campaign, run by strategist Wakita Hiroko, masterfully executed a "distancing" strategy. In PPO-held districts, campaign posters featured Honda alone with the "Stable Progress" slogan. In SCP-held districts, the PPO ran aggressive challenger campaigns. They emphasized policies like targeted tax incentives for small businesses and a temporary cap on fuel prices, directly contrasting them with the SCP's calls for broad, uncosted wealth taxes.

===The SCP's Defensive Campaign and Critical Gaffes===
Aoyagi's SCP campaign was defensive from the start, struggling to justify the 6.8% inflation rate and the schisms within its own government.

The campaign's "worst moment" occurred during a stump speech in a rural district. When a voter complained about the soaring price of rice, Prime Minister Aoyagi attempted to deflect, saying, "We are all making sacrifices. In my own home, we are eating more brown rice and millet to do our part." The comment, intended to show solidarity, was seen as incredibly out of touch. Opposition-aligned tabloids ran front-page headlines like "'''LET THEM EAT MILLET'''" and "'''Aoyagi's Rice Gaffe'''," which fatally damaged his "man of the people" image.

Aoyagi's most damaging moment came during the final KTK debate on July 20. LDP leader Tanizaki, in a rare effective attack, brought up Aoyagi's 2020 admission of increasing defense spending. Aoyagi became visibly agitated, responding, "We live in a dangerous world! Even a party of peace must be able to defend itself!" Honda Shigetsuma capitalized on this, calmly stating, "The Prime Minister promised our coalition partners in the Socialist Party that he would cut defense spending. He broke that promise. It makes you wonder what other promises he is willing to break." This exchange was seen as the final nail in the coffin for the OSP, whose voters abandoned the party in droves, feeling betrayed.

===The LDP's Stagnation and "Squabbling"===
The LDP, under Sadakazu Tanizaki, failed to capitalize on the government's clear weaknesses, with a campaign that was widely seen as disorganized and backward-looking.

The party's campaign was visibly hampered by the recent, bitter leadership recall election. Local LDP chapters were often more focused on supporting their faction (Tanizaki's or his rivals') than on winning new seats. This "continued squabbling" was a frequent media topic, with the ''Toyozawa Nichi Nichi Shinbun'' running an editorial titled "The LDP Fights Itself While Okaiken Burns."

In a desperate attempt to gain traction, the LDP ran a series of negative ads attempting to link PPO leader Honda Shigetsuma to the 2016 Paraña Bank Scandal. The ad was a failure. Voters largely associated the scandal with the LDP (who had been in power at the time), and the PPO immediately ran a counter-ad showing a 2016 clip of Honda himself calling for an investigation. The LDP pulled the ad within 48 hours, but it reinforced their image as a party stuck in the past.

The LDP's economic message was seen as incoherent. In some districts, they attacked the SCP for "reckless spending," while in others, they attacked the PPO for not spending ''enough'' on direct cash handouts, promising a 5% cut to the consumption tax without a clear plan to pay for it. This failure to present a unified, credible alternative allowed the PPO to capture the vast majority of disaffected centrist and conservative voters.

